    <body>
      <sec id="article-42051.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Migraine is a prevalent primary headache disorder marked by recurrent episodes of moderate to severe pain, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, photophobia, or phonophobia. Some patients experience an aura due to cortical spreading depression. The pathophysiology involves trigeminovascular activation, neurogenic inflammation, and cortical hyperexcitability. Standard management includes pharmacologic and behavioral therapies, yet a subset of patients develop chronic, drug-refractory migraine. For these individuals, advanced options such as botulinum toxin injections, peripheral nerve neurolysis or neurectomy, and neuromodulation with occipital nerve stimulation may be considered. Selection of appropriate surgical candidates requires careful clinical evaluation, recognition of trigger sites, and assessment of comorbidities to balance benefit with risks such as muscle weakness, neuropathic pain, or device-related complications.</p>
        <p>This course strengthens clinicians' knowledge and competence in the evaluation and management of refractory migraine. Learners explore the mechanisms, techniques, and evidence supporting advanced interventions, and they develop strategies for safe patient selection and complication prevention. The curriculum highlights interprofessional collaboration among neurologists, surgeons, psychologists, psychiatrists, pharmacists, and specialty-trained nurses. Each professional brings unique expertise, encompassing medication optimization, behavioral health, surgical planning, and patient education. Collaborative care improves diagnostic accuracy, procedural safety, and adherence, ultimately enhancing patient outcomes and quality of life.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess the clinical features, severity scoring, and comorbidities of patients with refractory migraine to determine candidacy for advanced interventions in collaboration with an interprofessional healthcare team.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate between pharmacologic, procedural, and neuromodulatory treatment options for migraine by interpreting available evidence and communicating effectively with other clinicians to guide shared decision-making.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based strategies for botulinum toxin injection, peripheral neurolysis, or neuromodulation while coordinating perioperative and postoperative care with nurses, pharmacists, and behavioral health specialists.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate patient outcomes following advanced migraine therapies and improve long-term management plans through structured communication and collaboration across the interprofessional healthcare team.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=42051&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=42051">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Migraine is a primary neurologic disorder characterized by recurrent headache, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, or vertigo, and may present with or without aura.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The condition&#x000a0;affects&#x000a0;11.7% of the United States population, including 17.1% of women and 5.6% of men.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Migraine can be episodic, acute, or chronic. Pharmacologic treatment includes &#x003b2;-blockers, anticonvulsants, calcium channel blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Migraine surgery is indicated when the condition is refractory to medical treatment. Chronic migraine is&#x000a0;defined by the International Headache Society classification of headache disorders, third edition:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Headache occurring &#x02265;15&#x02009;days/month.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Duration &#x0003e;&#x02009;3&#x02009;months.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other features of migraine occurring &#x02265;8&#x02009;days/month.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Earlier theories proposed&#x000a0;that migraine&#x000a0;was generated by vasodilation of the cerebral vasculature; however, this mechanism has been refuted by several&#x000a0;studies that showed an absence of migraine in response to vasoactive intestinal peptide, which increases blood flow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Similarly, medications that precipitate migraine, like sildenafil, do not cause sustained vascular changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, cortical and brainstem hyperexcitability may&#x000a0;contribute to&#x000a0;migraine pathophysiology,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;and cortical spreading depression is responsible for the aura.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Trigeminal afferents project to the meninges and release pain mediators such as substance P, calcitonin gene&#x02013;related peptide, and neurokinin A, leading to a cycle of neurogenic inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;The strongest clinical evidence supports a peripheral origin of migraine,&#x000a0;as demonstrated&#x000a0;by the beneficial effect of botulinum toxin and peripheral neurolysis for chronic migraine. Migraine surgery focuses on neurolysis of sensory branches of the trigeminal and occipital nerves innervating the face and occiput.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r10">[10]</xref> These nerves include the supraorbital, supratrochlear, zygomaticotemporal, auriculotemporal, and greater and lesser occipital nerves. Sometimes, the pain is also due to hypertrophic nasal turbinates or a deviated nasal septum that irritates branches of the trigeminal nerves.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Migraine surgery is indicated for a disease that is refractory to conservative measures, including medication and behavioral therapy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>The presence of a diagnosed psychiatric disorder is a relative contraindication to migraine surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s6" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>All patients being considered for migraine surgery should first undergo evaluation by a neurologist.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s7" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Migraine severity&#x000a0;is assessed&#x000a0;using several scales; the most widely used is the Migraine Disability Assessment questionnaire (MIDAS).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;MIDAS grading is as follows: 0 to 5, grade I (little or no disability), 6 to 10, grade II (mild disability); 11 to 20, grade III (moderate disability); and greater than 21, grade IV (severe disability). The initial treatment of a migraine&#x000a0;includes pharmacologic and behavioral therapies; however, the headaches&#x000a0;can&#x000a0;become refractory to these interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The <italic toggle="yes">International Classification of Headache Disorders</italic>, third edition (beta version), revised&#x000a0;the definition of a refractory migraine to include at least 8 monthly migraine headaches with 15 or more headache days a month (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Migraine Treatment Algorithm). When rhinogenic headache is a consideration, a computed tomography scan of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses is indicated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s8" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>
<bold>Botulinum Toxin</bold>
</p>
        <p>The bacterium&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Clostridium botulinum </italic>produces botulinum toxin,<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>paralyzing muscles by inhibiting acetylcholine's release&#x000a0;from presynaptic terminals. Botulinum toxin has 8 subtypes, A through H, of which type A is safe and effective for therapeutic use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Justinus Kerner first used botulinum toxin as&#x000a0;a medical therapeutic, which he called "sausage poison." He performed animal and human experiments, including on himself.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Alan Scott initially used&#x000a0;botulinum toxin to treat strabismus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Thereafter, the toxin was used in focal dystonias such as blepharospasm,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;cervical dystonia,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;oromandibular dystonia,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;laryngeal dystonia,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;hemifacial spasm,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;focal hand dystonia,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;spasticity, focal hyperhidrosis,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;and to diagnose and treat migraine trigger sites.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Botulinum toxin reduces migraine frequency and severity by decreasing the irritation of the peripheral nerves by paralyzing the triggering muscles and decreasing the release of inflammatory mediators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from several double-blind controlled trials&#x000a0;demonstrated a 50% reduction in frequency and severity of migraines at 3 months, with efficacy lasting 3 to 4 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;However, results from other studies&#x000a0;reported only modest superiority of botulinum toxin over placebo.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Although botulinum toxin can be used short-term as an acute and prophylactic treatment,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;long-term use is limited due to the development of resistance, diminished efficacy, and adverse effects such as muscle paralysis, atrophy, and irritation, especially with higher doses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Therefore, some experts recommend using it only as a screening tool before neurolysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Botulinum toxin&#x000a0;is injected into the pericranial muscles at multiple sites according to the location of the pain. The most commonly used safe and effective dose is 25 U in normal saline. This total dose can be divided into 3 U for each temporalis muscle (total, 6 U), 2.5 U at 4 frontalis muscle sites (5 U unilaterally; 10 U bilaterally), 3 U into each corrugator muscle (total, 6 U), and 3 U into the procerus muscle in the midline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;For occipital headaches, 12.5 to 50 U may be injected on each side of the midline near the greater and lesser occipital nerves. Pain relief lasting 6 to 12 weeks indicates that the patient is an appropriate surgical candidate.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Peripheral Neurolysis/Neurectomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>The beneficial effect of peripheral neurolysis of the supratrochlear and supraorbital nerves on migraine was first recognized when Guyuron found that his patients were relieved of a debilitating migraine&#x000a0;after brow lift surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Subsequently, the group published a prospective study confirming the findings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;Neurolysis is performed after identification of trigger sites and demonstration of greater than 50% pain relief for at least 6 weeks following botulinum toxin injection. Botulinum toxin is injected into the bilateral frontal, temporal, and occipital regions (12.5 U each) or the corrugator supercilii muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Evaluation for hypertrophied nasal turbinates irritating the trigeminal nerves of the nasal septum should also be performed. Peripheral neurectomy is&#x000a0;also effective for treating drug-refractory migraine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Frontal headache:</bold> An eyelid incision retracts the orbital septum caudally, and the orbicularis oculi is retracted cranially. The corrugator supercilii&#x000a0;and depressor supercilii are resected, and the nerves are freed. Both supraorbital and supratrochlear blood vessels are ligated, and the supraorbital foramen is unroofed. The space is filled with the medial orbital fat and fixed with 6-0 polyglactin 910 sutures. The incisions are then closed with 6-0 nylon sutures. This procedure can be performed bilaterally.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Anterior temporal headache:</bold> The treatment of anterior temporal headache consists of severing the zygomaticotemporal nerve. For a frontotemporal headache,&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;procedure is combined with that for frontal headaches by making 2 incisions, one in the upper tarsal crease and another at the superior temporal line.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;These procedures can also be performed endoscopically.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;The zygomaticotemporal nerve&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;located&#x000a0;16 mm lateral and 6 mm cranial to the lateral canthus. A 2 cm incision is made at this point, and dissection is performed between the 2 layers of the temporalis fascia. The nerve is severed and located at the lateral margin of the orbit. The distal aspect is buried deep in the muscle, and closure is performed in layers.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Posterior temporal headache:</bold>&#x000a0;The auriculotemporal nerve is the most common cause of this type of headache. A 2 cm incision is made at the base of the zygomatic arch. The nerve and superficial temporal blood vessels are identified, and the blood vessels are ligated. The nerve is isolated for 1 to 2 cm, severed, and buried in the temporalis muscle. The incision is closed in layers.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Occipital&#x000a0;headache:</bold>&#x000a0;In cases of occipital migraine, occipital nerve neurolysis from the semispinalis capitis muscle can be performed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;For diffuse occipital region pain, greater occipital neurolysis is performed. A midline incision is made from the external occipital protuberance to the C2 spinous process. The trapezius fascia is incised approximately 1 cm from the midline. The trapezius fibers are split in line, and dissection is performed below them to locate the greater occipital nerve. The semispinalis capitis muscle around the nerve is transected to free the nerve. Similarly, the trapezius muscle and fascia over the nerve are removed. The procedure can be repeated contralaterally. Pedicled fat is removed from the overlying skin, inserted between the nerve and muscle, and sutured. The incision is closed with 3-0 nylon sutures.</p>
        <p>For lateral occipital headache, lesser occipital neurolysis or neurectomy can be performed. A 2 cm incision is made about 2 cm medial to the mastoid in the hairline. The thick temporal fascia is incised. The lesser occipital nerve and blood vessel are dissected. The artery is coagulated and ligated. The nerve is dissected out for about 1 to 2 cm, severed, and buried in the muscle. The incision is closed with 3-0 nylon sutures. Occipital neurolysis offers immediate relief but also has a high rate of recurrence,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;although alternative options such as neuromodulation are available.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Rhinogenic headaches:</bold> If hypertrophied turbinates or a deviated nasal septum are identified as the cause of headache, turbinectomy or septoplasty is performed. The&#x000a0;branches of the maxillary nerve through the sphenopalatine ganglion mediate this type of headache, and botulinum toxin treatment is ineffective.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Neuromodulation for Migraine</bold>
</p>
        <p>Neuromodulation is&#x000a0;performed&#x000a0;if patients do not experience relief with medical management or if peripheral neurolysis is not feasible or beneficial. The initial option is occipital nerve stimulation,&#x000a0;which is achieved by&#x000a0;placing electrodes around cervical dorsal nerves in the suboccipital region and connecting them first to a trial stimulator. If greater than 50% pain relief is achieved, the electrodes are connected to a permanently implantable pulse generator. Other options for neuromodulation include sphenopalatine ganglion stimulation,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;supraorbital nerve stimulation,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;and vagus nerve stimulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Although these interventions have demonstrated&#x000a0;benefit in initial studies, large controlled trials are needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Occipital nerve stimulation modulates nociceptive transmission in the trigeminocervical complex, likely by activating large-diameter afferent fibers that inhibit pain signaling from small nociceptive fibers (gate control theory).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Occipital nerve stimulation&#x000a0;provides analgesia in the distribution of both occipital and trigeminal nerves through modulation of the trigeminocervical complex when the trigeminal nucleus and portions of the upper 3 cervical dorsal nerves are stimulated.&#x000a0;Approximately 30% to 50% of patients achieve greater&#x000a0;than 50% pain relief.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;This procedure has a high rate of lead readjustment (approximately 50%), but it is minimally invasive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;Occipital nerve stimulation&#x000a0;has also demonstrated benefit in&#x000a0;cluster headaches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Nerve Decompression</bold>
</p>
        <p>Decompression of nerves at the trigger site (frontal, temporal, occipital) by removing surrounding tissues, including muscles and blood vessels, can be helpful for patients with medically refractory, chronic migraine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r49">[49]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s9" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Botulinum Toxin</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients may experience&#x000a0;transient&#x000a0;atrophy of injected muscles, especially the temporalis muscle. The incidence rate can be as high as 23%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Other complications include transient eyelid ptosis when injections involve the frontalis muscle or neck muscle weakness when injections involve the cervical region.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Peripheral Neurolysis/Neurectomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients can have neuroma formation when neuromyotomy is performed, which can be prevented by burying the severed nerve end in the muscle. Infection and hemorrhage can occur, but the incidence is low.&#x000a0;Temporary paresthesia and pruritus at the&#x000a0;surgical&#x000a0;site are also possible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Turbinate Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postoperative complications include epistaxis, sinusitis, nasal dryness, or septal deviation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neuromodulation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lead migration can occur if not anchored properly. Infection is managed with antibiotics and rarely requires implant removal.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>A migraine is a common primary headache disorder. A neurologist evaluates clinical features and the MIDAS score to assess severity. Initial treatment includes pharmacologic and behavioral therapies. Botulinum toxin is commonly used to identify the trigger sites. Peripheral neurolysis or neurectomy is an effective treatment for drug-refractory cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s11" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Patients with migraines should be evaluated by an interprofessional team that includes a neurologist, psychologist, psychiatrist, surgeon, specialty-trained neurology nurse, and other healthcare professionals. Appropriate case selection is necessary for&#x000a0;a successful surgical outcome. A pharmacist should&#x000a0;assist with pharmaceutical selection and monitoring. Nurses should provide preoperative and postoperative patient education to ensure optimal care.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s12" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>Neurologists and neurosurgeons must continue to collaborate, refine surgical interventions for migraine, and establish effectiveness, thereby ensuring improved outcomes for patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-42051.r52">[52]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-42051.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
